## ABSTRACT
## KEYWORDS
metal–organic frameworks, single-atom catalysts, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ electroreduction, axial coordination environment
## Introduction
Single-atom catalysts (SACs), featured with isolated metal atoms dispersed  on  supports,  present  unprecedented  activity  and selectivity for various reactions and have become the frontier of heterogeneous catalysis [1–10]. Single metal atoms in SACs, with extremely  high  specific  surface  energy,  are  usually  stabilized  via coordination  interaction  by  suitable  supports  [11, 12].  Thereby, the  catalytic  performance  of  SACs  is  significantly  affected  by their  local  coordination  environments  [13–15].  Taking $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electroreduction  as  an  example,  it  suffers  from  low  efficiency due  to  chemical  inertia  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and  the  competing  hydrogen evolution,  and  the  decrease  of  coordination  number  for  singleatom  Ni  catalysts  has  been  widely  proved  to  alleviate  these challenges  [16–18].  On  account  of  this,  regulating  the coordination  environments  of  SACs  precisely  will  be  of great  significance  for  catalytic  performance  optimization  and mechanism investigation [19–22].
To  be  specific,  single  metal  atoms  implanted  nitrogen-doped carbon (M-N-C) catalysts, one of the most important SACs, are usually  identified  to  possess  porphyrin-like  planar $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ sites dominantly  [23, 24].  However,  the  high  electron/structure symmetry  of $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ sites  is  unfavorable  for  the  charge  transfer during catalysis, hindering the further performance optimization of  M-N-C  catalysts  [25].  To  break  the  symmetric  electronic structure  of  single-atom  metal  sites,  a  series  of  coordination environment regulation strategies, such as changing coordination numbers and replacing some of the coordinated nitrogen atoms by other heteroatoms, have been developed and greatly enhanced the  catalytic  performance  of  M-N-C  catalysts  [17, 26–28]. Unfortunately, these regulations are all limited in the carbon plane and unavoidably change the properties of carbon support in the meantime [29, 30]. The axial coordination environment regulation, which can be performed out of carbon support plane, should be an ideal way to optimize the catalytic performance of SACs without influencing the properties of carbon support [31]. Although  great  progress  has  been  achieved  in  the  precise construction  of  SACs,  the  axial  coordination  environment regulation remains a grand challenge [25, 32, 33].
Metal–organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  [34–37],  as  a  class  of crystalline  porous  material  with  tunable  components  and structure, exhibit great research prospects in diverse fields [38–43]. In  recent  years,  MOFs  have  been  regarded  as  one  of  the  most auspicious candidates for the accurate construction of SACs [44, 45]. Thanks to the flexible structure and composition, MOFs  also  show  great  advantages  on  the  coordination environment  regulation  of  single-atom  sites  via  one-step pyrolysis  [29, 46].  However,  due  to  the  uncontrollability  of pyrolysis  process,  traditional  one-step  pyrolysis  of  MOFs  is  still difficult to control the axial coordination of SACs accurately [33]. Inspired  by  post-metallization  strategy  for  the  construction  of metal porphyrin complexes, the axial coordination atoms can be easily adjusted by selecting metal salts with different anions [47]. Following this synthesis route, the post insertion of single atoms on MOF-derived nitrogen-doped carbon, which can decouple the carbon  support  preparation  and  subsequent  single  metal  atom decoration,  should  be  an  ideal  way  to  regulate  the  axial coordination environments of SACs.
## 2    Results and discussion
Taking $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ $\left(\mathrm{Cl}\right)$ as  an  example,  it  shows  inherited morphology and particle size of N-C (Figs. 2(c)–2(e) and Fig. S3 in  the  ESM).  No  obvious  nanocrystals  or  nanoparticles  can  be observed  in  the  TEM  image  of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C $(\mathrm{Cl})$ ,  manifesting  the atomic dispersion of Ni (Fig. 2(e)). Moreover, the powder X-ray diffraction  (XRD)  pattern  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  only  presents  two broad  peaks  corresponding  to  the  (002)  and  (101)  planes  of carbon and no crystalline species of Ni is detected, in accordance with TEM observation (Fig. S4 in the ESM and Fig. 2(e)). Raman spectrum  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  displays  two  peaks  at  1,339  and $1{,}578\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , which can be ascribed to the disorder (D band) and graphitic (G band) carbon, respectively (Fig. S5 in the ESM). The intensity  ratio  of  D  band  to $\mathrm{~G~}$ band $(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ for $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  is calculated to be $1.04,$ showing the similar graphitization degree to N-C (Fig. S5 in the ESM). Furthermore, $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl) exhibits a large surface area $(633~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ with highly porous structure similar to  that  of  N-C,  which  favors  the  dispersion  of  catalytic  center atoms and would expedite mass transfer during catalysis (Fig. S6 in the ESM). Meanwhile, $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ $({\mathrm{Br}})$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I) are also characterized  and  present  similar  morphology,  graphitization degree, and porous structure to those of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl) (Figs. S4–S7 in the ESM and Figs. 2(d) and 2(e)).
To identify the chemical compositions and element electronic states  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (X) $\mathrm{{(X=Cl,}}$ Br,  and  I),  X-ray  photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is further conducted. The Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peaks in $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}.$ - N-C (X) $\mathrm{(X=Cl,}$ Br, and I) exhibit the decreased binding energy in the sequence of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl), $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Br), and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I), which can be ascribed to the decreasing electronegativity from Cl to I (Fig. 3(a)). This manifests the higher oxidation state of Ni in $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  than  those  in $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C $(\mathrm{Br})$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I). Additionally,  it  should  be  noted  that  the  peak  at $875.2\mathrm{eV}$ overlapping with Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ in $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I) catalyst is the signal of $\mathrm{~I~}3\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ (Fig. 3(a)) [50]. The N 1s spectra of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (X) $\mathrm{(X=Cl,}$ Br, and I) can be deconvoluted into five characteristic peaks, and the existence of metal-N species, attributed to the empty orbitals of metal atoms accepting the lone pair electrons of N atoms, clearly indicates  the  formation  of  Ni–N  coordination  bonds  (Figs. S8(a)–S8(c)  and Fig. S9  in  the  ESM)  [51].  Furthermore,  the existence of halogen atoms in the three obtained catalysts is also confirmed by XPS measurements. The Cl 2p XPS spectrum of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ - N-C (Cl) presents a peak at $197.7\mathrm{eV}$ , manifesting the formation of Ni-Cl  coordination  (Fig. S8(d)  in  the  ESM)  [52].  Moreover,  the peaks at 68.0 and $68.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the Br 3d XPS spectrum of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Br)  and  the  peaks  located  at  619.2  and $630.7~\mathrm{eV}$ in  the  I  3d spectrum  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I)  verify  the  existence  of  Ni-Br  and  Ni-I coordination,  respectively  (Figs.  S8(e)  and  S8(f)  in  the  ESM) [53, 54].  The  aberration-corrected  high-angle  annular  dark-field scanning  transmission  electron  microscopy  (HAADF-STEM) image of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl) illustrates isolated bright spots dispersed on the  carbon  support,  manifesting  the  atomic  dispersion  of  Ni (Fig. 2(f)).  The  above  results  initially  confirm  the  formation  of single-atom  Ni  sites  coordinated  by  nitrogen  and  the corresponding halogen atoms.
Furthermore,  X-ray  absorption  spectroscopy  (XAS)  is employed  to  give  more  clear  evidence  on  the  coordination environments and electronic states of Ni atoms in $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (X) (X $=\mathrm{Cl}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , Br, and I) catalysts. It can be observed that the near-edge absorption energy of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl) shifts to higher value compared with Ni foil in the Ni K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectrum, indicating the positive valence state of Ni in $\mathrm{Ni_{1}\mathrm{-N\mathrm{-C}}}$ (Cl)  (Fig. 3(b)).  The  Fourier  transform-extended  X-ray absorption  fine  structure  (FT-EXAFS)  curve  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl) exhibits the dominated peak centered at $\sim1.60\mathring\mathrm{A},$ which could be attributed to the Ni-N/Cl coordination (Fig. 3(c)). In addition, the absence  of $\mathrm{\DeltaNi-Ni}$ scattering  path  (around $2.18\mathrm{~\AA~}$ )  suggests  the atomic dispersion of Ni atoms in $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ $(\mathrm{Cl})$ , agreeing well with the  results  of  HAADF-STEM  observation  (Figs.  3(c) and 2(f)). Moreover, the EXAFS curve fitting is further performed to acquire the coordination configuration of Ni atoms of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl). The best  fitting  result  demonstrates  that  the $\mathrm{Ni}$ atom  is  coordinated with four in-plane $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ atoms and an axial $\mathrm{\DeltaCl}$ atom (Fig. 3(d) and Table S2 in the ESM). In addition, the XAS analysis has also been performed  on $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ $({\mathrm{Br}})$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I).  The  Ni  K-edge XANES  spectra  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Br)  and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I)  suggest  the positive valence states of Ni in the two samples (Figs. S10(a) and S10(b)  in  the  ESM).  Furthermore,  the  best  EXAFS  curve  fitting results  for $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ $(\mathrm{Br})$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I)  show  their  similar coordination configurations to $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl), in which Ni atom is coordinated with four in-plane N atoms and an axial Br or I atom (Fig. S11 and Table S2 in the ESM).
Inspired  by  the  results  above,  electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction measurements have been conducted using $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (X) $\mathrm{(X=Cl,}$ Br, and I) as electrocatalysts. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves indicate that the current densities of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (X) $\mathrm{(X=Cl,}$ Br, and I) and N-C in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere are all higher than those in argon,  manifesting  their  favorable  activity  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})$ (Figs.  S12–S15  in  the  ESM).  In  addition,  the current  response  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere  is  much higher than those of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}\mathrm{-N\mathrm{-C}}}$ (Br), $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I), and N-C (Fig. 4(a) and Fig. S15 in the ESM). When tested at constant potentials, $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ - N-C  (Cl)  shows  the  supreme  CO  FEs  in  all  potentials  ranging from $-0.45$ to $-0.85\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. RHE) (Fig. 4(b)). Significantly, $\mathrm{Ni_{1}-N}.$ C  (Cl)  affords  a  maximum  CO  FE  of $94.7\%$ at $-0.7\mathrm{~V~}_{;}$ outperforming those of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Br) $(83.3\%$ at $-0.65\mathrm{V}.$ ), $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I) $69.4\%$ at $-0.65\mathrm{V}.$ ), and N-C $57.1\%$ at $-0.55\mathrm{V}.$ ) (Fig. 4(b) and Fig. S16 in the ESM). For all tested catalysts, CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are the main products, and no liquid products are identified from the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (1H NMR) results (Fig. 4(b) and Figs. S16–S18 in the ESM). Additionally, $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl) also exhibits a superior CO partial current density $\left({J_{\mathrm{{CO}}}}\right)$ to those of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Br) and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I),  further  manifesting  excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ performance of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl) (Fig. 4(c)). The Tafel slope of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}\mathrm{-N}.}$ - C  (Cl)  is $194~\mathrm{\mV/}$ dec,  lower  than  those  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Br) $(209\mathrm{mV}/\mathrm{dec})$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I) $(252\mathrm{mV}/\mathrm{dec})$ , revealing the fastest kinetics  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ (Fig. S19  in  the  ESM).  The double-layer  capacitances $\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ are  determined  to  be  28.9,  28.4, and $28.0\mathrm{mF/cm^{2}}$ for $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl), $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Br), and $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (I), respectively, indicating their similar electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) (Fig. S20 in the ESM). The similar ECSA as well as Ni loading  of  different  samples  further  prove  that  the  catalytic performance difference of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl), $\mathrm{Ni_{1}\mathrm{-N\mathrm{-C}\left(B r\right)}}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -NC (I) is actually caused by the different axial halide atoms (Fig. S20 and Table S1 in the ESM). Finally, the stability measurement of best-performed $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl) is conducted at $-0.7\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , and it can be seen that the FE of CO and current densities can be well retained during $^{\mathrm{10h,}}$ demonstrating the long-term stability of $\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -N-C (Cl) for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ (Fig. 4(d)).
Given  the  superb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ performance  of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ $(\mathrm{Cl})$ ,  it  is further employed as a cathode catalyst in the rechargeable $\mathrm{{Zn-CO}}_{2}$ battery  [51, 55].  The $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  is  immersed  in $0.8\mathrm{~M~CO}_{2}.$ - saturated ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ electrolyte. The $Z\mathrm{n}$ foil, serving as the anode, is put  in $0.8\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH  solution  containing  0.02  M $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}$ (Fig. 5(a)). During the discharge process, the cathode reaction is $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and the $Z\mathrm{n}$ anode converts into $\mathrm{Zn(OH)_{4}}^{2-}$ [55]. In the charge process, oxygen forms on the cathode and the anode reaction is the deposition of $Z\mathrm{n}$ [55]. As shown in the discharge and  charge  voltage  profiles, $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl)  exhibits  a  discharge potential of $0.426\mathrm{~V~}$ at $0.5~\mathrm{mA}$ , which still maintains at $0.175\mathrm{~V~}$ when $2.5\mathrm{\mA}$ discharge  current  is  applied  (Fig. 5(b)).  In  the meantime,  a  charge  potential  of $2.257\mathrm{~V~}$ results  from  a  charge current of $0.5~\mathrm{mA}$ , which increases slowly after $1\ \mathrm{mA}$ (Fig. 5(b)). Meanwhile, the selectivity of CO reaches $93.8\%$ at $2\mathrm{mA}$ during the discharge process and can be well retained in a wide range of discharge  current,  indicating  the  excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction performance of $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ $(\mathrm{Cl})$ (Fig. 5(c)). Moreover, $\mathrm{Ni_{1}{-}N{-}C}$ (Cl) displays  stable  voltage  under  consecutively  discharge/charge  at $2.0\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for $24\ \mathrm{h},$ suggesting  the  excellent  stability  of  the $\mathbf{Z}\mathrm{n-CO}_{2}$ cell (Fig. 5(d)).
